1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,306
Your brain is like a massive, complex symphony orchestra,

2
00:00:06,406 --> 00:00:10,250
with billions of neurons firing in perfect harmony.

3
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,234
To listen to this music, scientists

4
00:00:13,334 --> 00:00:17,175
and doctors use Electroencephalography, or EEG.

5
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:22,734
They place tiny microphones—called electrodes or channels—across the scalp

6
00:00:22,834 --> 00:00:26,175
to record the electrical melodies of the mind.

7
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:29,938
But recording the brain is messy. In the real world,

8
00:00:30,038 --> 00:00:34,325
patients move, wires get bumped, and sensors lose connection.

9
00:00:34,466 --> 00:00:38,491
Suddenly, our beautiful symphony is interrupted by harsh

10
00:00:38,591 --> 00:00:42,616
static, or worse—complete silence from missing channels.

11
00:00:42,733 --> 00:00:46,891
We want to use artificial intelligence to decode these

12
00:00:46,991 --> 00:00:52,333
signals to help diagnose diseases or build brain-computer interfaces.

13
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:54,949
But there is a major bottleneck.

14
00:00:55,049 --> 00:00:58,375
Traditional AI models are incredibly rigid.

15
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:02,895
They require a fixed 'seating chart.' If you hand them a recording

16
00:01:02,995 --> 00:01:07,800
with missing channels or a different electrode layout, the model breaks.

